<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constantine XI Palaiologos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1453">1453</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Constantine XI · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palaiologos-dynasty">Palaiologos dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Constantine XI Palaiologos was the last emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. He died in 1453 defending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> against the Ottoman army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a>. His death and the fall of the city ended the Byzantine state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constantine-xi-palaiologos.jpg" alt="Constantine XI Palaiologos" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ILoveHirasawaYui · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Konstantinos_XI_Palaiologos_fresco_(less_edited).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Constantine XI</b>'s death fighting on the walls when Mehmed II took Constantinople on 29 May 1453 — the last Roman emperor dying in defence of the last Roman city — was one of history's most symbolically complete endings, closing eleven centuries of continuous institutional existence with a final…</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1428 <b>Constantine Palaiologos</b>, the future last Byzantine emperor, gained Glarentza by marrying a niece of Carlo I Tocco, and in 1429–1430 he took Patras from its Latin archbishop.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After a 53-day siege, Mehmed II's army broke through the city walls; <b>Constantine XI</b> died in the fighting.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-29">29 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the final collapse of the principality, Chalandritsa was taken by the Byzantines of the Despotate of the Morea, under <b>Constantine Palaiologos</b>, in 1429, at the same time as the campaign that won Patras.</blockquote><a href="/state/barony-of-chalandritsa">Barony of Chalandritsa</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1429 <b>Constantine Palaiologos</b>, future last Byzantine emperor, took Patras from Archbishop Pandolfo Malatesta's officials, and the citadel surrendered in 1430.</blockquote><a href="/state/latin-archbishopric-of-patras">Latin Archbishopric of Patras</a></li><li><blockquote>Konstantin's daughter Helena married the Byzantine emperor Manuel II and was the mother of the last emperor, <b>Constantine XI</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-dejanovic">Lordship of Dejanović</a></li></ul></section>
